## ABSTRACT
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) exhibit attractive properties such as highly accessible surface area, large porosity, tunable pore size, and built-in redox-active metal sites. They may serve as excellent candidates to construct implantable flexible devices for biochemical sensing due to their high thermal and solution stability. However, MOFs-based sensors have only been mostly reported for in-vitro chemical sensing, their use in implantable chemical sensing and combination with flexible electronics to achieve excellent mechanical compatibility with tissues and organs has rarely been summarized. This paper systematically reviews the biochemical sensors based on MOFs and discusses the feasibility to achieve implantable biochemical sensing through MOFsbased flexible electronics. The properties of MOFs and underlying mechanisms have been introduced, followed by a summarization of different biochemical sensing applications. Strategies to integrate MOFs with flexible devices have been supplied from the standpoints of matching mechanics and compatible fabrication processes. Issues that should be addressed in developing flexible MOFs sensors and potential solutions have also been provided, followed by the perspective for future applications of flexible MOFs sensors. This paper may serve as a reference to offer potential guidelines for the development of flexible MOFs-based biochemical sensors that may benefit future applications in personal healthcare, disease diagnosis and treatment, and fundamental study of various biological processes.
## KEYWORDS
flexible electronics, metal-organic frameworks, biosensors, chemical sensing, implantable devices
## Introduction
The appearance of flexible electronics that is compatible with soft, curvilinear bio-tissue has enabled numerous applications in health monitoring and disease diagnosis due to its superior mechanical properties and diverse functions [1–7]. The advantages of flexible devices for biochemical sensing involve minimized tissue damage and conformable tissue contact [8, 9], leading to high sensitivity and repeatability in comparison with traditional rigid devices that are incompatible with the soft mechanics of tissue. Flexible electronic devices have been demonstrated for measuring various biophysical parameters such as temperature [6, 10], heart rate [11–13], hydration [14, 15], blood oxygen [16, 17], and biopotential [18, 19]. In addition, flexible chemical sensors for glucose [20–22], lactate [23, 24], and electrolytes [5, 6, 25] have also been developed. However, flexible devices for implantable biochemical sensing are still in their early stages, not to mention the devices capable of longterm, multi-parameter measurement with exceptional sensitivity and stability.
A strategy to achieve sensitive and stable chemical sensing is to introduce functional materials such as bioactive materials [26, 27], metal nanoparticles (NPs) [28, 29], metal oxide [30, 31], and 2-dimensional (2D) materials [32, 33]. These materials offer excellent structural advantages and unique sensing mechanisms, and exhibit different levels of adaptability in in-vivo measurement. For example, the bioactive materials represented by enzymes and antibodies are difficult to work in extreme environment and the activity of bioactive materials may degrade rapidly over time [34–37]. On the other hand, conventional non-enzymatic materials possess simple composition and long lifespan, but issues such as lack of functional groups, simple spatial configurations, and limited structural variation lead to unsatisfied sensing performance in terms of physical size selectivity, binding specificity, and enrichment of analytes. Besides, requirements of alkaline working environment for most metal oxide sensors confine their potential as biochemical sensors for in-vivo applications. Thus, novel functional materials that offer stable sensing capability, high tunability, and biocompatibility demand in-depth investigation.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are formed by inorganic metal ions or clusters (e.g., alkaline earth metals [38, 39], transition metals [40–42], and rare earth metals [43–45]) coordinated with organic ligands (e.g., carboxylates [46–48], phosphonate [49, 50], and sulfonate [51, 52]). Since the first MOF was demonstrated in 1989 [53], MOFs have been rapidly developed in terms of framework structures, functional groups, and potential applications in the last decades due to the probably infinite combination of coordination. Meanwhile, MOFs possess a high degree of synthetic versatility [54], which makes it possible to precisely tune the electrical, optical, magnetical, and mechanical properties of MOFs. For instance, MOFs are generally considered to be insulators with poor conductivity [55]. However, MOFs that are semiconductive [56], proton-conductive [57, 58], ionconductive [59], and ferroelectric [60] have also been achieved by selecting certain metal ions and tailored organic ligands [61–63], tuning framework structures [64], or introducing other functional materials into the pores [65, 66]. As a result, a series of MOFs with unique structures and attractive properties have been demonstrated, such as ZIF-8 [67, 68], UiO-66 [69], MOF-5 [70], HKUST-1 [71], and MIL-101 [72, 73]. They exhibit excellent properties such as highly accessible surface area, large porosity, tunable pore size, and built-in redox-active metal sites, resulting in promising applications in gas storage [74–76], catalysis [77–79], drug release [80–82] and chemical sensing [83–85]. They may serve as excellent candidates for in-vivo chemical sensing due to their high thermal and solution stability. However, MOFs-based sensors have only been mostly reported for in-vitro chemical sensing, their use in implantable chemical sensing and combination with flexible electronics to achieve excellent mechanical compatibility with tissues and organs have rarely been summarized.
Here, we review the biochemical sensors based on MOFs and discuss the feasibility of implantable biochemical sensing using flexible electronics integrated with MOFs. The properties of MOFs and underlying mechanisms have been briefly introduced, followed by systematical summarization of different biochemical sensing applications. Based on the advantages of flexible electronics for implantable biochemical sensing, mechanical properties of MOFs, and corresponding miniaturization strategies, approaches to integrate with flexible substrates have been outlined (Fig. 1). Issues that should be addressed in developing flexible MOFs sensors and potential solutions have been provided, followed by the perspective for future applications of flexible MOFs sensors. This paper not only offers comprehensive reviews of general mechanisms of MOFs in biochemical sensing, but also serves as a reference to supply general guidelines for the development of flexible MOFs-based biochemical sensors that may benefit future applications in personal healthcare, disease diagnosis and treatment, and fundamental study of various biological processes.
## 2 Integration of MOFs and flexible electronics for implantable biochemical sensing
## 2.1    Properties and sensing mechanisms of MOFs
MOFs are highly ordered and porous crystalline materials achieved through connecting metal ions/clusters with various organic ligands. The diversities of metal ions and organic ligands lead to different types and hierarchies of inorganic/ organic hybrid frameworks, and further influence the functions and performances of MOFs. The resulting unique structural, optical, chemical, electrical and biocompatible properties have been summarized in the following sections.
## 2.1.1    Structural properties
MOFs are porous crystalline materials with various structural properties, such as dimensionality, pore size (micropore, mesopore, and macropore), morphology, and some other derived properties (e.g., specific surface area and porosity). MOFs can be categorized into zero-dimensional (0D) [86], one-dimensional (1D), 2D and three-dimensional (3D) frameworks according to the spreading direction of coordination bonds. 1D MOFs are featured with zigzag chains [87], tubular chains [88], and helical chains [89] in which coordination bonds spread only in one direction. 2D MOFs are composed of stacked layers superimposed in fully aligned or staggered manners. Some common structures include rectangular [90–93], Kagome [94], honeycomb [95], and rime-like type [96]. 3D MOFs are generally highly porous and stable with coordination bonds extending in three directions, resulting in 3D pillared layers or interpenetrated grids with various structures such as cubical [97], diamond-shaped [98], and octahedral [99] structures. Topologically, secondary building units (SBUs) have been adopted to predict and describe the structures of MOFs. Predetermined networks are formed by assembling inorganic and organic SBUs with various geometries under specific reaction conditions [100, 101]. For instance, MOF-2 has been synthesized using a solvothermal approach [102]. During the synthesis process, pairs of $\mathrm{ZnO_{4}}$ tetrahedra are bridged by four carboxylate C atoms to form dinuclear SBUs, which are then joined together by benzene links to generate a 2D coordination network. MOF-5 that possesses a 3D network is constructed by connecting benzene links with octahedral SBUs that contain four $\mathrm{ZnO_{4}}$ tetrahedra with a common vertex and six carboxylate C atoms [103]. Meanwhile, the properties of MOFs are highly structure-dependent. Al-based MOFs with different shapes (e.g., hexagonal bipyramids, triangular sheets, and hollow spheres) and sizes were controllably synthesized using a microwave-assisted solvothermal approach for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. Results showed that the capture capacity of the hexagonal bipyramidal MOFs was enhanced with increased particle sizes, surface area, and pore volume. Besides, the capture capacity of hollow spheres $(6.96\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}})$ ) was greater than triangular sheets $(5.35\mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}})$ ) and hexagonal bipyramidal crystals $\left(3.32{-}6.17~\mathrm{mmol{\cdot}g^{-1}}\right)$ ), indicating that the shape of the MOFs plays a major role in the capability of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture [104].
Furthermore, MOFs are notable for their large specific surface area and high porosity. Compared with other materials, the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of MOFs can reach $\sim7,000\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [105], which is much higher than other porous materials such as zeolites $(<800~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ [106–109], porous metals/metal oxides $(<150~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ [110–118], and carbons (e.g., activated carbon $(<2,400\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ [119–121], carbon nanotubes $(<600~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ [122, 123], graphene oxide $(<350~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ [124–127], and graphene $(<620~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ [124, 127]). The large surface area of MOFs can be utilized to support large amounts of catalytically active sites, while the porosity of MOFs allows facile diffusion of the targeted analytes to access the active sites [128]. In addition, the large surface area of MOFs also enables enrichment of analytes through multiple mechanisms such as “molecular sieving effect”, open metal site interaction, hydrogen bonding, or $\pi{-}\pi$ affinity. [129–132]. Besides, porous MOFs can load metal NPs through post-modification approaches to further improve the catalytic performance [124, 133–135], leading to strong interaction of substances in nano-size pores in MOFs due to the confinement effect [134, 136, 137]. A luminescent MOF with a BET surface area of $1{,}467~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a pore volume of $0.88\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was designed for cholesterol sensing [138]. The presented sensor exhibited excellent sensing performance with good reversibility and a detection limit of $0.82~\upmu\mathrm{M}.$ , which can be attributed to the high surface area and large pore size that offer a large number of accessible active sites for cholesterol. A porous Ni-MOF/CNTs composite was fabricated via a self-assembly method and was used as an efficient electrocatalytic oxidation material for glucose detection [139]. The presented electrochemical sensor showed a high sensitivity of $13.85\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{mM}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , a low detection limit of $0.82~\upmu\mathrm{M}.$ , and a wide linear range of $1~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}{-}1.6~\mathrm{mM}$ due to the highly porous structure that facilitates the enrichment and electrocatalysis towards glucose. Thus, the unique structural properties of MOFs can be introduced into biochemical sensors for implantable biosensing with enhanced sensitivity and selectivity.
## 2.1.2    Optical properties
## 2.1.3 Chemical properties
Chemical performance of MOFs can be regulated by changing the types of metal ions (e.g., Ca, Mg, Fe, Mn, Al, $\mathrm{Zr,}$ and Ti) and organic ligands (e.g., dicarboxylic acid, tricarboxylic acid, tetracarboxylic acid, and imidazolate) or functionalizing organic ligands with different groups [178]. For example, organic ligands and unsaturated metal sites that serve as Lewis acidic sites in MOFs can connect with acidic (–OH, –COOH, $-S\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{H},$ ) and basic (–N, –NH, $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) groups, resulting in capabilities to catalyze organic oxidation, couple small molecules, decompose water pollutants, and convert $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to useful compounds (e.g., methanol, formic acid, methyl, carboxylic acid, and carbonic ester) [179–185]. Moreover, multiple functional groups of MOFs can be used to specifically integrate with targets via chemical coupling for improving the sensing performance. A 2D microporous Tb-based MOF containing uncoordinated carboxyl groups that point to the interior of pores was synthesized [186]. Owing to the uncoordinated carboxyl groups that can serve as a host to bind foreign metal ions, the MOF has been used to measure $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ with high sensitivity and selectivity via a luminescence quenching mechanism.
The chemical stability of MOFs is the prerequisite for constructing implantable biochemical sensors. The chemical stability of MOFs is mainly determined by the bonding strength between the carboxylate groups and the metal sites. According to Pearson’s hard/soft acid/base principle, the interaction between the hard Lewis base (high electronegativity) and the soft Lewis acid (low valence state) is much weaker than that between the hard Lewis base and the hard Lewis acid (high valence state) [128, 187]. As the carboxylate-based ligands are regarded as hard bases, MOFs composed of metal ions (e.g., $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , and ${{\mathrm{Ni}}^{2+}}$ , etc.) with lower valence states show poor chemical stability in aqueous media when coordinated with the carboxylate-based ligands [128, 188–193]. Notably, the MOF constructed by Cr-based sites and carboxylate-based ligands was an exception. Due to the water stability and redox activity of the $\mathrm{Cr}$ -based sites, these MOFs have been utilized in electrochemical sensing [194, 195]. In contrast, group IV metal ions, such as $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ , $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ , and ${\mathrm{Hf}}^{q+}$ , possess exceptional chemical and thermal stability [196] due to their high valence states. For example, UiO-66 has excellent chemical stability in acidic and neutral aqueous solutions with a wide range of pH values [196–200]. Compared to the carboxylatebased MOFs, MOFs constructed from nitrogen-containing anionic ligands such as ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 may achieve better chemical stability in water. In addition, pyrazolate or triazolebased MOFs also exhibit high chemical stability, especially for those operating in alkaline media or phosphate-containing biological environment [128].
## 2.1.4    Electrical properties
The electrical conductivity and electronic structure of MOFs that possess highly ordered crystal structures can be interpreted by both band theory and charge carrier hopping. The band transport mode yields higher charge mobility and higher conductivity than the hopping mode [201]. A striking spatial and energetic overlap between symmetric orbitals is demanded by both transport modes. Whereas, poorer orbital overlaps can lead to higher interaction and lower mobility. Furthermore, a high density of charge scattering sites will reduce carrier mobility to the specific site. Despite the conductivity of the MOFs may be explained by both the band theory and charge hopping, the detailed mechanisms have not yet been systematically studied. In fact, most MOFs behave as insulators with poor conductivity $(<10^{-10}\mathsf{S{cdot}c m^{-1}})$ and low electron mobility $(<1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ due to the composition of hard metal ions and redox innocent organic ligands such as amino acids, imidazole, and aliphatic carboxylic acids, all of which are lack of conjugated systems to form effective pathways for charge transport [202, 203]. Various researches that aim to develop more conductive MOFs have been conducted [55, 203–209].
The guest-induced conductivity approach has been used to convert insulating MOFs to conductive MOFs through infiltrating conductive guests, such as metal NPs, nanoclusters, and conductive organic polymers [210–212], or non-conductive guests like $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and 7,7,8,8-tetracyanoquinododimethane (c) into MOFs [213–216]. For example, TCNQ was integrated with HKUST $\mathrm{(Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{BTC}=1,3,5$ -benzenetricarboxylate) to bridge the binuclear copper paddlewheels in the framework, resulting in strong electronic coupling between the dimeric Cu subunits. As a result, the highest conductivity of $0.07~\mathrm{S}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which was over six orders of magnitude increase than the pure HKUST has been achieved [216]. Similarly, a novel electrochemical sensor was obtained by immobilizing $_{\mathrm{Au-SH-SiO_{2}}}$ nanoparticles on $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOFs for L-cysteine sensing [217]. The presented sensor exhibited a strong electrocatalytic behavior towards L-cysteine with a linear range of $0.02\mathrm{-}300~\mathrm{mM}$ and a detection limit of $0.008~\mathrm{mM}$ The increase in oxidation current can be attributed to the porosity of an Au-SH- $S_{\mathrm{i}}\mathrm{O}_{2}@\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOF film and its enhanced electrocatalytic behavior that increases the electron transfer rate.
Another strategy to achieve conductive MOFs is to design intrinsically conductive frameworks that supply through-space and through-bond conduction. In the through-space approach, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction between electroactive moieties in the frameworks offers conduction paths for charge transport. The most common conductive MOFs based on through-space conduction are constructed by tetrathiafulvalene (TTF) ligands, which have also been widely used for preparing charge transfer salts and organic conductors [218–221]. Park et al. synthesized isostructural $\mathbf{M}_{2}$ (TTFTB)-based MOFs, which exhibit a high correlation between the single-crystal conductivities and the shortest S···S interaction defined by neighboring TTF cores [222]. An electrochemical glucose sensor was developed based on a redox-active 3D MOF $\mathrm{([Mn_{2}\{N i(C_{2}S_{2}(C_{6}H_{4}\bar{C}O O)_{2})_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]\cdot2}}$ DMF), which was isomorphic to the $\mathrm{\small{[Mn_{2}(T T F T B)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}}$ [223]. The fabricated sensor showed improved sensing performance with a high sensitivity of $27.9\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\mathrm{M}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and a low detection limit of $1.0\times10^{-7}\mathrm{M}$ due to the multiple oxidation-reduction states that can oxide glucose to glucolactone by the high oxidation state $\mathrm{[NiS_{4}]}$ center. Besides of TTF, isophthalate-based ligands can also yield conductive MOFs in which close $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction between ligands can induce high charge carrier mobilities [221]. For the through-bond conductivity mechanism, the charge transport happens between covalently bonded conjugated organic ligands and metal sites that possess loosely bound electrons. In addition, orbital symmetry and energy overlap between metals and ligands further promote charge transport and conductivity [201]. Moreover, the conductivity of MOFs can be modulated by the mixed valence conductivity, which is caused by the intervalence charge transfer between oxidizable metal sites [224– 226]. For example, the single-crystal conductivity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{0.67}\mathrm{BDT})_{3}{\cdot}17$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}){\cdot}0.5(^{\mathrm{i}}\mathrm{Pr}\mathrm{OH})$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{\bar{B}D T}=5,5^{\prime}$ -(1,4-phenylene) bis (1Htetrazole)) $(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{BDT})_{3})$ can be tuned over five orders of magnitude by varying the extent of mixed valence of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ As a result, the conductivity can be up to $1.8\mathsf{S}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ [226, 227]. Furthermore, the bonding between aromatic ligands and transition metal ions can generate strong hybridization of the $\pi{-}\mathrm{d}$ orbitals, resulting in improved charge transport and conductivity. Lastly, easily polarizable atoms that possess lower electronegativity and better orbital overlap offer higher conductivity, thus ligands that contain S donors form highly polarized coordinate bonds with metal sites, resulting in higher conductivity than their O and N equivalents [201]. A 3D Eu-based MOF with a high proton conductivity of $2.73\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S{\cdot}c m^{-1}}$ was used as a sensitive luminescent sensor for ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions in aqueous solutions [228]. The high conductivity of the presented MOF can be attributed to fast proton propagation through a hydrogen-bonding network that contains dimethyl ammonium, coordinated water, and lattice water molecules.
In addition, 2D $\pi$ -conjugated MOFs that are formed by neutral 2D planar layers composed of divalent metal ions linked to aromatic or polyaromatic ligands have been observed to possess high conductivity and high charge carrier mobility [201, 229]. The layered structure can cause strong in-plane charge delocalization and extended $\pi\cdot$ -conjugation along the metal-organic sheets. In this regard, Wang et al. successfully used highly conductive $(\sim2{,}500~\mathrm{S{{·}}c m^{-1}})$ ) and transparent $(82\%)$ Cu-BHT films as alternatives to ITO electrodes [230]. Novel conductive phthalocyanine $(\mathrm{Pc})$ -based 2D MOFs made of different metals coordinated to the central cavity and the terminal groups of the ligands were reported. These MOFs contained planar layers with square pores that were stacked in a completely eclipsed mode. Basing on this structure, a novel NiPc-2D MOF with a conductivity of $0.2\mathsf{S}\mathsf{{·}}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ was synthesized to serve not only as a connecting unit but also as an electrocatalytically active site for catalysis [231–235]. A 2D conductive MOF has been synthesized as functional materials for neurochemicals sensing [236]. The good electrical conductivity can be attributed to the use of hexasubstituted triphenylene conjugated frameworks that can improve the electron transfer rate. The proposed MOFs sensor showed excellent sensitivity towards dopamine and serotonin with LODs of $63\pm11~\mathrm{{nM}}$ and $40\pm17~\mathrm{{nM}}$ , respectively, and remarkable reversibility up to 100 cycles.
## 2.1.5 Biocompatibility
The compositions of MOFs and their crystal sizes as well as the solvents used during synthesis are major factors for the biocompatibility of MOFs [237–239]. For instance, terephthalic acid as a widely used ligand showed low toxicity with a median lethal dose of more than $1\ {\mathrm{g}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{kg}}^{-1}$ [240], which is much larger than the dose used in practical operations. Meanwhile, a large number of biological endogenous organic ligands (e.g., amino acids, polysaccharides, peptides, amino acids, proteins, and porphyrins) have also been used to improve the biocompatibility of MOFs [241–245]. On the other hand, Tamames-Tabar et al. studied in-vitro cytotoxicity of up to $14~\mathrm{MOFs}$ with different metals and structures [246]. The results showed that Zn-based MOFs are considered to be the most cytotoxic, followed by $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs and Fe-based MOFs. However, no difference in cytotoxicity has been observed within different polymorphs, indicating that the MOFs topology has a negligible effect on the cytotoxicity of MOFs. Besides, the solvents that generally possess high toxicity can be entirely removed by simultaneous heat and vacuum treatment, leading to imperceptible influence on biocompatibility. Besides, the crystal sizes can affect the distribution and the metabolism of MOFs in-vivo. Research suggests that NPs between 20 and $30~\mathrm{nm}$ can be metabolized by the kidney, while NPs 30 and $150~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameters can be mostly swallowed by the mononuclear phagocytic cells in bone marrow, kidneys, hearts, and stomachs. The NPs between 150 and $300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ were mainly concentrated in spleens and livers [247–249]. Thus, the biocompatibility of MOFs can be improved by tuning the size of MOFs [250–252].
So far, the biocompatibility evaluation of MOFs mainly focuses on the in-vitro cytotoxicity studies through adding MOFs into different macrophages or other cell lines [178]. Among in-vitro cytotoxicity tests, MTT [7], the alamarBlue® cell viability assay [253], and CCK-8 [254] have been widely performed. A summary of cytotoxicity tests for different kinds of neat MOFs has been shown in Table 1, in which cell viability higher than $70\%$ has been observed, satisfying the recommended value by USP (ISO 10993-5) [255]. Despite lots of demonstrations in in-vitro cytotoxicity of MOFs, their in-vivo toxicity has rarely been reported. Some rare examples involve evaluating the histological sections and analyzing the biophysiological parameters of experimental animals [256–258]. The in-vitro and in-vivo toxicity assessments show that various MOFs are biocompatible to cells and tissues, suggesting promising applications in drug delivery [244, 257], biochemical sensing [259–261], and disease treatment [262, 263].
## 2.2    Preliminary  applications  and  challenges  of  in‐vitro chemical sensing using MOFs‐based electronics
Owing to the unique properties mentioned above, the potential of MOFs as functional materials in chemical sensors has been explored in various applications, such as environmental monitoring, food safety control, biomedicine, disease diagnostics, warfare agent detection, and industrial process management. Various analytes (e.g., gas, pH, ions, biomolecules, and cells) have been detected based on different sensing mechanisms, such as electrochemistry, photoluminescence, and colorimetry.
## 2.2.1 $p H$ sensing
MOFs can respond to different pH values through changes in fluorescent intensity (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)) [275, 276]. The sensing mechanism is primarily based on the protonation/ deprotonation of ligands, especially for MOFs that contain N atoms on their skeletons [277]. Zhou and coworkers reported a porphyrinic-based MOF (PCN-225) that can be used to measure $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ values in an extensive range of 0–10.2 (Fig. 3(c)) [278]. When the $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ is less than 5, the gradual protonation of the pyridine nitrogen destroys the conjugated p-electron system of the porphyrin core, making the MOF hardly emit light under acidic conditions. At the same time, due to the deprotonation of the imino group under alkaline conditions $\mathrm{(pH>7)}$ , the conjugated system is restored, resulting in a sharp increase in fluorescence emission. The MOFs-based pH sensors may be used for in-vivo tumor microenvironment monitoring and electrolyte balance assessment, which demand a measurement range from 6.8 to 7.6 [279–281].
## 2.2.2    Ion sensing
MOFs can also be used to detect cations. Abdelhamid et al. constructed water stable and porous La-MOF NPs, which had large 1D channels $(11.3\mathrm{~\AA~})$ to allow easy attachment of small molecules. $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ can be detected in environment with $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ values ranging from 6 to 12. The linear range of detection was from 16.6 to $167\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , and the LOD value was $16.6\upmu\mathrm{M}$ [285]. A chiral Tb-based MOF has also been used to detect $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ . The detection mechanism can be attributed to a strong quenching effect on the fluorescence due to the interaction of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ with the chiral helical channels of the MOF (Fig. 3(e)) [286]. Sun and co-workers reported a series of luminescent Cd-based MOFs decorated with chelating $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ sites for $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ detection. The absorption of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ between 230 and $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ hindered the absorption of MOFs, resulting in attenuation of photoluminescence of MOFs [287]. Qian and coworkers further increased the surface areas of MOFs by developing 2D MOFs nanosheets that supplied more accessible active sites than those in bulk-size MOFs, resulting in improved sensing efficiency of $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ with a low LOD of $0.45\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and a fast response time of 2 s [288]. From the above examples, most ion sensing mechanisms utilized the unique optical properties of MOFs, while the selectivity of different ions was ensured through adjustable porous structures. Thus, more investigations based on other sensing mechanisms with improved selectivity are still demanded to improve the diversity of MOFs-based ion sensors.
## 2.2.3 Biomolecules
Glucose as a vital energy supplying molecule has been detected through various enzymic or non-enzymic approaches. Among them, conventional electrochemical sensors based on glucose oxidase or glucose dehydrogenase offer high sensitivity and selectivity. However, they also possess inevitable shortcomings such as instability, poor reproducibility, and high cost. Enzymefree glucose sensors based on precious metals (Au, Pt), metal alloys (Pt-Ni), transition metals $\mathrm{(Cu,Co)}$ , and metal oxides $(\mathrm{CuO})$ have been developed with long-term stability, however, lack of specificity and the requirement of alkaline medium limit the use of such sensors in biological environment. In addition, precious-metal-based electrodes are easily poisoned by adsorbed interferences (e.g., chloride), and the oxidation kinetics is too slow for practical applications. Further modifications through nanostructurization and incorporating other metals were proposed to overcome the drawbacks [295, 296], but issues such as low enrichment efficiency, poor selectivity, and dependence on alkaline environment remain unsolved. The diverse metal sites and abundant functional groups of MOFs are beneficial for developing multifunctional sensors. The porous structures in MOFs may promote the enrichment of targets, while tunable pore structures may increase selectivity. In addition, some Cu-based MOFs are capable to create localized alkaline environment, minimizing the impact of environmental pH on sensing [289].
MOFs-based glucose-sensing approaches may use MOFs as electrocatalysts, immobilization carriers, or precursors of electrocatalysts [297–299]. A Cu-catecholate (Cu-CAT) MOF was synthesized to develop a glucose biosensor for human perspiration monitoring. The MOF exhibited a stable glucose sensitivity that was invariable to the pH values of external environment due to the unique fractal structures of the MOF. This also means that the MOF can directly work in acidic sweat, unlike other Cu-based MOFs, which require alkaline conditions to detect glucose (Fig. 3(f)) [289].
In order to further enhance the selectivity of glucose sensing, glucose oxidase (GOx) has been integrated to the MOFs through absorption, covalent linkage, pore encapsulation, and coprecipitation [300–302]. The MOFs/natural enzyme hybrids combined the advantages of controllable catalytic activity of the MOFs and selectivity of natural enzymes [303]. Furthermore, the natural enzyme immobilized in MOFs can improve its tolerance to varying $\mathrm{\ttpH}$ and temperature [304]. Xu et al. synthesized Fe-MOF as a carrier for immobilizing GOx via covalent binding. The Fe-MOF-GOx hybrids not only exhibited higher stability and reusability than purely mixing Fe-MOF and free $\mathrm{GOx},$ , but also showed a wide linear range $(1-500~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ and a low detection limit of $0.487\upmu\mathrm{M}$ for glucose detection [303]. The hierarchical, porous structures of MOFs (HP-MIL-88B-BA) also offered sufficient recognition sites to facilitate GOx immobilization and prevent enzyme leakage. The hierarchical porosity increased the mass transfer rate and reduced the response time of glucose (Fig. $3(\mathrm{g)}^{\cdot}$ ) [290].
The sensitivity of the glucose-sensing can be improved by combining MOFs with excellent electrochemical activity and materials with good conductivity. Wang et al. designed a novel 3D flower-like Ni-terephthalic acid (Ni (TPA)) MOF doped with single-walled carbon nanotubes (SWCNT) (Fig. 3(h)) [291]. The nanocomposites that were coated onto glassy carbon electrodes exhibited remarkably strong electrocatalytic activity toward glucose oxidation with a wide linear range from $20\upmu\mathrm{M}$ to $4.4~\mathrm{mM}$ , a low detection limit of $4.6~\upmu\mathrm{M},$ and fast response time of less than $5s_{\scriptscriptstyle{\mathscr{O}}}$ . In comparison with other traditional nonenzymatic materials for glucose detection, the MOFs with unique morphological features possess a large BET surface area $(430.76~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , rich redox-active sites, and suitable pore sizes for charge transfer among molecules. A self-supported $\mathrm{CuNi/C}$ electrode has been obtained by electrodepositing CuNPs on a Ni-based MOF. The electrode was utilized as a non-enzymatic sensor that can be used to measure glucose in blood serum. The sensor provided a high sensitivity of $17.12\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{mM}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2};$ , a low detection limit of $66.67\ \mathrm{nM}$ , and a wide linear range from 0.20 to $2.72\mathrm{mM}$ [297]. The proposed sensor exhibited very high sensitivity compared with other non-enzymatic sensors [296, 305–307]. Other advantages of the sensor included good reusability, reproducibility, and stability, suggesting that $\mathrm{CuNi/C}$ electrodes were good alternatives for long-term glucose detection.
Other small biomolecules such as uric acid (UA), amino acids, and neurotransmitters have also been measured by MOFsbased sensors. Jia and co-workers constructed a fluorescent biosensor based on 2-picolinic acid-modified UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ to detect UA (Fig. 3(i)). The sensing principle was based on the fluorescence quenching effect of MOFs via hydrogen bonding, coordination, and $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction with UA [292]. The resulting linear detection range was within $0.01{-}400~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ with a detection limit of $2.3~\mathrm{nM}$ . Lin et al. proposed an enantioselective sensor for amino alcohol detection based on chiral fluorescent Cd-based MOFs, which possess high Stern−Volmer constants from 490 to $31{,}200\mathrm{M}^{-1}$ and enantioselectivity ratios from 1.17 to 3.12. The fluorescence of the MOFs was quenched by chiral amino alcohols through the H-bonding between the amino alcohol and the binaphthol moieties within the MOFs. The preconcentration effect inside the MOFs channels further enhanced the sensitivity [308]. A ratiometric probe based on a dual-emission Eu-MOF was constructed and showed great potential in rapid detection of aspartic acid and glutamic acid with low LODs of 21 and $22.4~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , respectively. The fluorescent quenching by amino acids was due to the rapid coordination of water molecules and the slight elongation of $\mathrm{Eu-O}$ bonds, resulting in weakened energy transfer from ligands to ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ [309]. Zhang and co-workers developed an electrochemical sensor based on a glassy carbon electrode modified with polypyrrole (PPy)/ZIF-67/Nafion hybrid for dopamine (DA) detection. The sensor exhibited high sensitivity $^{'}1.656\ \upmu\mathrm{A}{\cdot}\upmu\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ , good reproducibility, and long-term stability for DA in both medicine and human serum [310]. The efficient enrichment of MOFs can benefit in-vivo detection of trace amounts of small molecules. In a word, functionalized groups and tunable pore sizes of MOFs can increase the selectivity for different small molecules in complex biological environment.
MOFs with tunable pore features via changing the different terminal ligands were designed to bind recognition molecules (aptamer or antibody) for identifying proteins and DNA. Du and co-workers synthesized three types of electrochemical $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOFs sensors with tunable pore sizes for lysozyme detection, These sensors showed a wide detection range from 0.005 to $1\ \mathrm{ng}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ and a low detection limit of $3.6~\mathrm{pg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ [311]. MOFs can non-covalently interact with single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) through electrostatic bonding, $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, or hydrogen bonding. The formed ssD ${\mathrm{NA@MOF}}$ can be used as an effective and selective sensing platform for detecting complementary DNA and RNA sequences based on the fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET). Chen et al. have synthesized water-stable 3D Dy-based MOFs for RNA detection with a detection limit of $160~\mathrm{pM}$ [312]. Jiang and coworkers developed an amine-functionalized MOF (UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ), which interacted with fluorophore-modified ssDNA and quenched its fluorescence. When the complementary target ssDNA combined with existing ssDNA on MOFs, the resulting double-stranded DNA detached from the UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2};$ and the fluorescence was recovered due to the absence of the FRET process [313]. Besides of fluorescent detection, electrochemical sensors for DNA/RNA detection have been developed through entrapping functional molecules in MOFs with mimetic catalytic activity. Lei and co-workers designed an electrochemical sensor for DNA detection based on the mimetic catalysis of FeTCPP $\scriptstyle{\ @\mathrm{MOF}}$ and an allosteric switch of hairpin DNA (Fig. 3(j)). The FeTCPP $@$ MOF was synthesized and conjugated with streptavidin (SA) as a recognition element. The presence of target DNA triggered the unfolding of hairpin DNA and the formation of free SA aptamers, leading to the deposition of FeTCPP $@$ MOF-SA onto the electrode surface and measurable electrochemical signal. Due to specific dual biorecognition of DNA and aptamers, the method could discriminate target DNA from mismatched oligonucleotide and complex environment [293]. Wang et al. synthesized AuNPs modified $\mathrm{cu}$ -MOFs and utilized it for highly sensitive microRNA (miRNA) detection in a range from $1.0\mathrm{fM}$ to $10~\mathrm{{nM}}$ with a detection limit of $0.35\mathrm{fM}.$ . The Cu-MOFs increased the catalytic effect and provided a larger surface area for enveloping AuNPs and immobilizing ssDNA. The prepared ssDNA-AuNPs@Cu-MOFs nanocomposite was linked to the modified electrode via chain hybridization to induce signal amplification [314]. The excellent signal amplification effect of MOFs ensures the extremely high sensitivity of MOFs-based macromolecular sensors. In addition, MOFs can also serve as carriers for aptamers or antibodies to achieve high-throughput in-vivo biomarker monitoring.
## 2.2.4 Cells
Based on the high specific surface areas and diversified chemical functional groups, MOFs have also been applied as signal amplification components to couple with specific recognition molecules for capturing and detecting cells related to disease diagnosis and therapy. Chen and coworkers designed a sandwichtype electrochemical biosensor for detecting human breast cancer cells (MCF-7 cells). The DNA tetrahedron linked with dual-aptamers was immobilized onto the gold electrode surface for specific recognitions. The PCN-224, a kind of porphyrin-based $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF, was used as a nanocarrier, which was modified with PtNPs through an in-situ reduction method to form PCN-224-Pt nanoelectrocatalysts. Then the PCN-224-Pt, G-quadruplex/hemin (GQH) DNAzyme, and horseradish peroxidase (HRP) served together as signal amplifiers. Experimental results showed that the linear detection range of the biosensor was from 20 to $1\times10^{7}$ cells $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ with a detection limit of 6 cells· $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ (Fig. 3(k)) [294]. Wu and coworkers developed a simple, low-cost, and high-efficient impedance cytosensor based on folic acid (FA)-functionalized $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOFs (UiO-66). The electrochemical impedance results indicated that $\mathrm{FA@UiO–66}$ nanocomposite exhibited good selectivity in distinguishing folate receptors (FAR)-rich cancer cells (e.g., HeLa and H1299 cells) and FAR-negative cells (e.g., HEK-293 cells) [315]. Du and coworkers used a MOF-on-MOF approach to design a bimetallic core–shell Tb Fe-MOFs nanostructure, which was utilized as a platform for immobilizing carbohydrate antigen 125 (CA125) aptamer to detect CA125 and MCF-7 cells with a low detection limit of $58~\upmu\mathrm{U\cdotmL^{-1}}$ and 19 cells·mL−1, respectively[316]. MOFs-based cell sensors make it possible to capture and intervene tumor cells at the cellular level in-vivo.
## 3 Compatibility of MOFs and flexible electronics in terms of mechanical properties
## 3.1    Mechanical properties of MOFs thin film
The investigation of mechanical properties of MOFs as functional materials in flexible electronics is essential since mechanical deformation may occur during the fabrication processes and practical operation. Under mechanical loading, the mismatch of mechanical properties between MOFs and flexible substrates may lead to structural damage represented by collapse in frameworks [317, 318], cracks in thin films [319, 320], and delamination between layers (Fig. 4) [321, 322]. The behaviors of MOFs under mechanical loading have been quantified through both theoretical studies (densityfunctional theory and molecular dynamics) and experiments (nanoindentation, high-pressure crystallography, atomic force microscopy (AFM), and spectroscopic ellipsometry) [323]. Due to the hybrid nature of MOFs, their elastic moduli that range from several GPa to tens of GPa fall between inorganic materials and polymers (Fig. 5).
The high porosity of MOFs may influence the mechanical properties. For example, MOF-5 with a BET surface area of $\sim2{,}800\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a pore size of $\sim3\:\mathrm{nm}$ has been measured using a nanoindentation technique by probing non-cracked regions within the crystals. A hardness value of $162\pm14\mathrm{{\:MPa}}$ and a modulus of $6.3\pm0.45$ GPa have been observed [324], both of which were relatively lower than that of other MOFs. The results indicated that MOF-5 is compressible and by far one of the softest frameworks [323]. Similarly, the mechanical properties of zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) have been characterized using single-crystal nanoindentation. The elastic modulus was found to be from 3 to $10\mathrm{GPa},$ and the hardness was determined to be from $300\mathrm{MPa}$ to 1.1 GPa [325]. It has been found that the elastic modulus of ZIFs showed a significant inverse correlation with the available void space inside, wherein highly porous frameworks such as ZIF-8, ZIF-20, and ZIF-68 exhibited relatively lower stiffness. Their results also showed that sterically bulky imidazolate-type linkage led to higher stiffnesses due to rigid ligand–ligand interaction. Besides the porosity, other features such as anisotropy, texture, and grain boundaries of MOFs thin films may also potentially influence many mechanical properties. For instance, oriented HKUST-1 thin films fabricated by liquid-phase epitaxy had an elastic modulus of $9.3\ \mathrm{GPa}$ and a hardness of 0.23 GPa. Both of the values agreed well with theoretical results [326]. However, HKUST-1 thin films synthesized by electrochemical approaches exhibited random orientations, resulting in a smaller elastic modulus of $3.5\mathrm{GPa}$ and a smaller hardness of 0.17 GPa than the oriented one [327].
Besides of intrinsic material properties, changes in the lattice parameters of some MOFs in response to physical or chemical stimuli, such as temperature, mechanical pressure, guest adsorption, and light absorption, may result in strain within the interfacial layers [328]. For instance, metals such as Au, Cu, and Ag [329, 330], as well as polymers such as polyimide [331], polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) [332] and polyethylene terephthalate (PET) [333] used in flexible electronic devices exhibit positive thermal expansion. However, a number of MOFs such as MOF-5 [334, 335] and HKUST-1 [336–338] were recently discovered to have an exceptionally large negative thermal expansion coefficient. As a result, the mismatch of the thermal expansion coefficient between the MOFs thin films and other substrates can produce residual stresses during the fabrication processes, causing defects in the materials and even delamination between layers [339]. Therefore, it is important to address the issue of thermal expansion behaviors by minimizing temperature fluctuations during film growth and device fabrication.
The capability of MOFs-based flexible devices in withstanding mechanical deformation has been characterized by several tests (Table 2). Li and co-workers presented a high-quality HKUST-1 nano-film on flexible gold-coated PET substrates using a liquid phase epitaxy approach for flexible memristors [340]. A uniform and reproducible resistance switching effect has been observed in stacked Au/HKUST-1/Au/PET layers, and can be well preserved during dynamic and static bending tests (Figs. 6(a) and $6(\mathrm{b})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ). When the bending rate was increased from 0.25 to $2\ \mathrm{mm}{\cdot}s^{-1}$ , the $I{-}V$ response remained almost unchanged. Resistance switching has been cycled more than 30 times at each bending radius or strain level. At a strain level of
$2.8\%$ , HKUST-1 memristor can still work normally, demonstrating better mechanical properties than most inorganic materials [341]. The authors pointed out that MOFs nano-films, which combined the mechanical flexibility of organic materials and the environmental stability of inorganic substances, possessed great potential as an information storage medium in wearable electronic devices in the future. Chen and co-workers demonstrated an alcohol-mediated resistance-switching behavior in MOFs-based memristors using a ZIF-8 film as a dielectric layer [342]. The ZIF-8-based memristors with Ag/ZIF-8/Si or Ag/ZIF-8/Au/PDMS/PET multilayer structures were fabricated via room-temperature growth of the ZIF-8 film with a thickness of $60\ \mathrm{nm}$ onto a silicon wafer or Au electrodes. The endurance tests of the memory showed that under repeated voltage scanning or continuous read voltages, the high-resistance state and the low-resistance state were well separated, and the on/off ratio of resistance remained as large as $10^{7}$ . The environmental responsivity and guest-modulated tuning of the current–voltage characteristics were almost unchanged under different bending radii when integrating the device on a flexible substrate (Figs. 6(c) and 6(d)), making the chemically mediated memristor a candidate in flexible and wearable electronics for nonvolatile memory applications. Pan et al. reported a MOFs-based flexible strain sensor with accurate signal detection and noise-screening properties [214]. An $\mathrm{I}_{2}@\mathrm{CuTCA}$ nanofilm was synthesized by chelating redox-active tricarboxytriphenyl amine (H3TCA) ligands with divalent ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ cations to create soft skeletons and doping with iodine to form a semiconductive charge-transfer sensor. The current of the sensor increased exponentially when the strain changed from $2.5\%$ to $3.3\%$ . An ultrahigh gauge factor of 11,200 has been observed at a strain level of $3.3\%$ (Figs. 6(e) and 6(f)). Ling et al. presented a novel Cu-MOF with 2D layered topology integrated onto flexible electrodes for electrochemical detection [319]. The proposed flexible sensors have been used to detect ascorbic acid, hydrogen peroxide, and L-histidine with detection limits of 2.94, 4.1 and $5.3~\upmu\mathrm{M};$ respectively. And the linear ranges and sensitivity of the sensors compared favorably with other sensors based on rigid platforms.
The flexibility of the MOFs sensors was characterized by bending tests (Figs. $6(\mathrm{g)}$ and $6(\mathrm{h})_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ ). The results showed that the MOFs membrane can withstand extreme deformation with a curvature smaller than $0.5~\mathrm{mm^{-1}}$ , suggesting favorable compatibility with flexible substrates. A non-enzymatic electrochemical sensor based on electrochemical water splitting-assisted electrocatalysis was developed by Zhu et al. for wearable perspiration glucose analysis [343]. Mild bending tests of the ${\mathrm{Pd}}\ @{\mathrm{ZIF}}.$ - 67-based flexible sensor were conducted. The results showed a relative standard deviation (RSD) of $1.89\%$ for 10,000 times mild bending, indicating an excellent mechanical performance of the sensor (Figs. 6(i) and $6(\mathrm{j})$ ). More recently, Zhou et al. presented a template-method to synthesize a hierarchical structure of MOFs hybrid arrays (MHA) on copper meshes (MHA@Mesh) for flexible tactile sensors [344]. MHA $@$ Mesh-based flexible sensors offered a rapid response rate $(<\mathrm{~1~ms})$ and a high sensitivity (up to $307\mathrm{kPa}^{-1},$ ) that was 20 times higher than that of MHA $@$ Foil-based sensors $(15\mathrm{{kPa}^{-1})}$ . The flexible pressure sensors could be applied to monitor finger motion and pulses without compromising their sensing performance during bending (Figs. $6(\mathrm{k)}$ and $6(1)$ ). These results indicated that MOFs thin films possess the capability of withstanding external mechanical bending without compromising their sensing performance, suggesting that these types of sensors can be applied to in-vivo sensing in extremely constrained space such as ureters, blood vessels, and subarachnoid space, or applications such as cortical electrophysiology mapping, spatiotemporal cardiac measurement, and bladder function monitoring in which large bending is required.
## 3.2    Integration of MOFs and flexible electronics
Due to large elastic moduli, MOFs are typically classified as brittle and rigid materials, which are mechanically incompatible with flexible polymer substrates. Thus, stress induced by deformation may lead to delamination of stacked layers and cracks in MOFs films, resulting in failure of the sensors [354]. An approach for integrating brittle and rigid inorganic materials with flexible electronic devices involves downsizing them into NPs and depositing or transferring them onto flexible substrates in thin-film formats. Such an approach has been widely demonstrated in several brittle materials including siliconbased materials [355–357], metals [8, 358, 359], and magnetic materials [360–362]. Similar approach can also be applied for integrating MOFs with flexible electronic devices.
## 3.2.1    Approaches for downsizing MOFs
Integration of MOFs with flexible electronic devices may start with downsizing MOFs particles into micro/nano-particles, which not only can preserve the same pore structures and surface reactivity as the bulky materials, but also can achieve MOFs thin films that match the mechanical properties of flexible electronics. The downsizing methods can be categorized into top-down and bottom-up approaches [363–367].
## 3.2.1.1    Top‐down approaches
Top-down methods typically involve using mechanical forces generated by mechanical grinding and ultrasonication to reduce the bulky grains into micro/nanocrystals. These approaches can effectively eliminate contamination during the downsizing processes as no addition is required [367]. Mechanical grinding has been considered to be the simplest and safest approach to generate MOFs thin films [7, 319, 368–371]. Banerjee and coworkers reported Gd-1,4-bis(5-carboxy-1H-benzimidazole-2yl)benzene) (Gd-pDBI) grown in $_{\mathrm{H_{2}O/D M F}}$ at $110~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ and then downsized to nanoscales through mechanical grinding to yield MG-Gd-pDBI particles for cancer-cell-specific drug release (Figs. 7(a) and 7(b)) [368]. The resulting nanosized MGGd-pDBI particles that possess rod shapes were $120~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in size. These particles offered excellent water dispersibility $(>90~\mathrm{min})$ , low blood toxicity, and high drug loading $(12\mathrm{~wt.\%})$ ), suggesting that such a simple mechanical grinding approach can yield MOFs nanocrystals without compromising their structural integrity, crystallinity, and porosity. Kitagawa et al. presented a gateopening switching behavior of a highly oriented crystalline MOFs $(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{py})_{2}[\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{CN})_{4}])$ thin film that consists of interdigitated 2D layers [370]. An obvious vapor-sorption behavior was observed after mechanically grinding the as-synthesized bulk MOFs $(135\mathrm{nm})$ into smaller crystals $(8.9\mathrm{nm})$ using a mortar, indicating that this type of gate-opening behavior in the thin film can be attributed to the crystal-downsizing effect. Wang et al. presented a ball-milling strategy to synthesize MOFs-based composites in which polyoxometalates (POMs) were enclosed in the cages of ZIFs for 5-fluorouracil release [372]. Ball milling was used to mix zinc oxide, 2-methylimidazole, and POMs using steel balls with a diameter of $10\mathrm{mm}$ at $40\ \mathrm{Hz}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ during which POMs were captured into the ZIFs through the force from mechanical grinding. The resulting composite allowed guest molecules to diffuse in and out, resulting in a methylene blue absorption of $126\mathrm{mg{\cdotg^{-1}}}$ that was much higher than $13.3\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ absorbed by solvothermally synthesized $\mathrm{POMs}/$ ZIF-8. A process to exfoliate layered MOFs into nanosheets without damaging the in-plane structure of MOFs was also developed [373]. $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(b i m)_{4}}$ crystals were first ball-milled at a very low speed of $60~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , followed by sonication in a mixture of methanol and $\mathfrak{n}$ -propanol for $30~\mathrm{min}$ , resulting in ultrathin nanosheets with a large lateral dimension of $1.5~\mathrm{mm}$ and a uniform thickness of $1.12\mathrm{nm}$ (Figs. 7(c) and 7(d)).
Ultrasonication is another emerging approach to downsize bulk particles to smaller scales and has been employed for a number of 2D nanomaterials, such as exfoliated graphene [374, 375] and delaminated MXenes [376, 377]. Ultrasonication has also been applied to obtain nanosized MOFs. Li et al. used ultrasonic delamination to turn bulk crystals of a layered MOF, $\{\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{TPA})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{DMF}\}_{n}$ into nanosheets [378]. By ultrasonicating the dried powder in acetone for about $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ at room temperature, MOFs nanosheets with thicknesses of $\sim1.5\:\mathrm{nm}$ and lateral dimensions around $200\mathrm{nm}\times300\mathrm{nm}$ have been successfully achieved. The resulting MOFs nanosheets exhibited remarkable amine intercalation property and reversibility, indicating that ultrasonication is a promising approach for fabricating MOFs nanosheets. Vittal and coworkers presented a molecular scissoring method to convert facile 3D Ln-MOFs into 2D structures [379]. Five Ln-MOFs with primitive cubic topology were exfoliated into 2D coordination polymer nanoplatelets with an estimated thickness under $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ via sonication with a power of $280~\mathrm{W}$ for $10\mathrm{~h~}$ in N,N-dimethylaniline (DMA). Another work of $\mathrm{Xu}$ et al. reported the exfoliation of 2D polyoxometalate-encapsulating MOF (POM-Co-MOF) into atomically thick nanosheets for highly efficient desulfurization application (Figs. 7(e) and 7(f)) [380]. The mixture was ball milled in liquid at a speed of $200\mathrm{rpm}$ for $90~\mathrm{min}$ , followed by exfoliation through sonication for $16\mathrm{h}$ . The delaminated nanosheets exhibited a 14-fold higher desulfurization efficiency and superior desulfurization performance as compared with bulk POM-Co-MOF.
## 3.2.1.2    Bottom‐up methods
The crystal sizes of MOFs can also be controlled by the nucleation rates [384]. External energy sources such as microwave [385, 386] and sonochemical methods [387, 388] have been used to promote nucleation during the synthesis processes of MOFs. It has been reported that the modification of solvent composition that can tune the hydrogen bonding interaction between the solvent and the ligand, [389, 390] or the addition of nucleators [391, 392] can also increase the nucleation rate of MOFs, resulting in decreased crystal sizes. Slower formation of MOFs has been achieved by reducing seed aggregation through tuning the synthesis parameters such as synthesis time [393, 394] and temperature [381, 395] during the crystal growth stage.
MOFs formation can be simplified as ligand substitution reactions on the metal sites [396]. The sizes of the MOFs are governed by the nucleation rate and the growth rate, and are time-dependent [393]. Thus, the simplest method to downsize MOF crystal is to control the kinetics of crystal formation, such as precursor concentrations, synthesis durations, and temperature. Carreon and coworkers reported the structural formation of ZIF-8 as a function of time at room temperature [393]. Different phases such as nucleation, crystallization, growth, and stationary during ZIF-8 formation have been investigated. Due to the Ostwald ripening that dissociates small clusters into free molecules and further redeposits them on the larger particles [397], the results showed that the average particle size increased significantly from $\sim50$ to $\sim500\ \mathrm{nm}$ with the increase in the crystallization time from $10~\mathrm{min}$ to $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The effect of varying both the crystallization temperature and time on the crystal growth of MOFs has also been explored [381]. Varying the synthesis time of Fe-MIL-88A crystals resulted in a gradual evolution of MOFs crystals from undefined morphologies with a size of $\sim100\ \mathrm{nm}$ to bulky rods with sizes $>1,000\ \mathrm{nm}$ after extending the reaction time to $24\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figs. $7(\mathrm{g)}$ and $7(\mathrm{h})$ ). The decrease in the synthesis duration resulted in insufficient time for the Fe-MIL-88A crystals to evolve into large, rod-like crystals, leading to smaller particles with inhomogeneous morphologies. A lower reaction temperature of $65^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for crystals synthesized between 0.5 and $24\mathrm{~h~}$ also resulted in relatively smaller particle sizes ranging from 110 to $1{,}050\mathrm{nm}$ .
The microwave-assisted method has been used to synthesize micro/nanosized MOFs due to its ability to provide localized power to the precursor solution, resulting in concentrated power and short reaction time [385]. Chang et al. reported the facile synthesis of nanoporous Cr-MIL-101 using microwave irradiation at $210^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for different crystallization times from 1 to $40~\mathrm{min}$ [382]. It has been found that the crystal size increased from $\sim40$ to $\sim200\ \mathrm{nm}$ with increased microwave irradiation time (Fig. 7(i)). The synthesized NPs exhibited an intricate structure and huge, rapid adsorption towards benzene, suggesting great potential for removing harmful organic contaminants. Doan et al. presented a strategy for synthesizing nano UiO-66 frameworks based on the microwave-assisted method at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ $90~\mathrm{W}$ for $3\mathrm{min}$ under an atmospheric condition, resulting in enhanced synthetic efficiency and particle homogeneity [398]. In-vitro curcumin adsorption measurement exhibited higher absorption efficiency of the nano UiO-66 frameworks in comparison to the reported UiO-66 synthesized by the solvothermal method.
Ultrasound is another alternative energy source that has been used to promote enhanced crystallization kinetics and phase selectivity [399]. The crystallization mechanisms of the HKUST-1 during the sonochemical synthesis process have been revealed by Mu et al [400]. A competitive mechanism that includes constructive crystal growth and deconstructive ultrasoundinduced fragmentation was proposed to describe the growth of MOFs. The results indicated that sonication power and choices of solvent were primary factors to determine the particle sizes, while sizes of sonicator tips and reactors contributed to the particle size distribution. Two novel Zn-based fluorinated metal-organic frameworks (F-MOFs) were synthesized through ultrasonic irradiation with a power of 12 W for various periods of time (15 and $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ ) at room temperature. The results indicated that increasing the ultrasonic irradiation time and concentrations can lead to the formation of smaller nanostructures and changes in morphology [401].
Microwave and ultrasound-assisted approaches can significantly shorten the synthesis time of MOFs to several minutes, allowing the rapid and efficient synthesis of MOFs in comparison with traditional hydrothermal or solvothermal approaches. However, there is also insufficient knowledge about heating parameters that are essential for precisely controlling reaction energy and MOFs sizes. For instance, the power coupling efficiency and the electric field distribution within the heating cavity determined by the dielectric properties of the reaction mixture need further investigation [402].
Mechanochemical synthesis is another alternative approach that provides energy through mechanical grinding, during which the chemical reactivity of the bulk reactant can be achieved by applying mechanical force [403, 404]. HKUST-1 crystals were synthesized using the mechanochemical approach by ball milling the precursors for $25~\mathrm{min}$ [405]. The resulting HKUST-1 crystals exhibited a size of $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which was significantly smaller than the bulk crystals, while the high specific surface area of $1{,}713\ \mathrm{\m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was similar to other HKUST-1 crystals. An efficient mechanochemical approach was proposed for synthesizing MOF-5 [383]. By grinding the reactant mixture of $\mathrm{{Zn}(O A c)_{2}.2H_{2}O}$ and $\mathrm{H_{2}B D C}$ with a molar composition of 3:1 for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ at $1,100\ \mathrm{r{\cdot}m i n^{-1}}$ , a BET area of $3,465.9~\mathrm{\m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ can be achieved to yield better adsorption towards long alkanes than short alkanes at low pressure, making MOF-5 a potential candidate for adsorption and separation of the hydrocarbons (Fig. 7(j)).
## 3.2.2    Integration of MOFs with flexible substrates
Downsized MOFs micro/nanocrystals can be transferred onto the substrates, either in colloidal suspension or powder formats. Sanchez and coworkers presented a simple route for the preparation of homogeneous MIL-101(Cr) films with high optical quality by a dip-coating technique [406]. A stable and homogenous colloidal solution of MIL-101( $\mathrm{\mathop{Cr}})$ NPs $22{\mathrm{nm}},$ ) was first prepared, followed by dip-coating it on a bare silicon wafer. The resulting film thickness ranging from 48 to $70\ \mathrm{nm}$ was found to be dependent on the concentration of the suspension [407]. This group also presented a dip-coating approach for the preparation of iron carboxylate MIL-89 thin films, which were $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ in thickness after depositing one layer of colloid. In addition, gels of MIL-89 can be formed after introducing iron acetate precursor for $10\mathrm{min}$ at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and monolithic xerogels can be obtained after three months at room temperature [408]. A spin-coating technique was also applied to deposit ZIF-8 colloidal suspension onto a silicon substrate, resulting in ZIF-8 thin films with different thicknesses between 70 and $200\mathrm{nm}$ [409]. Yoon et al. demonstrated a spray coating approach to deposit thin films of ZIF-8 onto glass and copper substrates. This highspeed deposition approach with a particle velocity of $\sim500\ \mathrm{m}{\cdot}s^{-1}$ was capable of generating either a textured crystalline film or a randomly oriented polycrystalline coating with tunable processing parameters (Fig. 8(a)) [410]. The solvent–air interface or the boundary layer between immiscible solvents can be used to grow free-standing MOFs films, which can then be transferred to other substrates. Thin films of $\mathrm{Cu}$ -BHT were prepared via a liquid–liquid interface reaction and then transferred onto glass substrates, resulting in large-area thin films composed of highly oriented nanosheets for field-effect transistors (FETs) fabrication (Fig. 8(b)) [411]. Free-standing porous $\mathrm{{Ni}}_{3}(\mathrm{{HITP})_{2}}$ films have been successfully prepared using an air-liquid interfacial self-assembly process (Fig. 8(c)) [412]. The as-prepared membrane that served as the channel layer of FET was transferred onto a $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Si}$ substrate using a simple stamping process. Our group also demonstrated that a drop-casting technique can be used to deposit MOFs onto flexible substrates [7, 319]. Highly viscous and homogenous MOFs dispersion was obtained by mixing grinded MOFs micro/nano particles with phosphate buffered saline (PBS) premixed with Nafion or chitosan, and then sonicated for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ using a high power sonicator. The resulting solution was drop-casted onto a flexible electrode to form an ultra-thin sensing layer. The proposed sensors have demonstrated excellent sensing performance in determining different biological targets with good specificity, reversibility, and longevity. The MOFs films can also be deposited and patterned using photolithography and acid etching [413, 414], both of which are compatible with the fabrication process of flexible electronics (Figs. 8(d) and 8(e)).
MOFs thin films can also be deposited on various flexible substrates such as plastics, papers, and textiles using an inkjet printing technique [415]. HKUST-1 thin films have been deposited onto flexible substrates through a precursor-based strategy, which used dimethylsulfoxide as the precursor solution, and ethanol/ethylene glycol as the solvent. HKUST-1 thin films with various thicknesses can be readily obtained by replicated printing and drying under $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{min}$ , followed by immersing the as-printed patterns into methanol for 30 min to remove the residual solvent (Figs. $8(\mathrm{f}){-}8(\mathrm{h}))$ . Junior et al. also demonstrated a low-cost and time-efficient approach for depositing photoluminescent Ln-MOFs on paper, plastic foils, and other flexible substrates with a conventional commercially available inkjet printer (Figs. $8(\mathrm{i}){-}8(\mathrm{k}))$ [416]. The ink solution composed of $\mathrm{\small{[Ln_{2}(M e l l)\cdot6H_{2}O]}}$ was placed in an inkjet cartridge, while $90\%$ ethanol was added in another cartridge as the precipitant. The deposition was performed using a single printing cycle with a resolution of $1{,}200\ \mathrm{\dpi},$ followed by drying for $5\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature, resulting in a thin film composed of Ln-MOFs microcrystals with sizes ranging from 0.4 to $8.0~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . These results suggest that the ink-jet printing technique allows fabricating large area, high density, and laterally patterned MOFs onto different flexible substrates in a low cost and time-efficient manner, which may be further combined with various functions of flexible electronic devices for wearable and implantable biochemical sensing.
## 3.2.3    Applications in biological sensing using MOFs‐based flexible electronics
The integration of MOFs with flexible electronics has been demonstrated in several literatures, most of which were based on physical signal detection. For instance, a solution-processed pressure sensor based on CuTCNQ nanowire arrays has been developed by Fu et al. to monitor radial artery pressure [349]. The presented sensor exhibited excellent sensing performance with a sensitivity of $6.25\mathrm{kPa}^{-1}$ , a detection range from 0 to $3\mathrm{kPa},$ and a detection limit of $0.73\mathrm{\Pa}$ (Figs. 9(a) and 9(b)). Simchi and coworkers presented a novel flexible piezoelectric sensor based on a poly(vinylidenefluoride) (PVDF) nanofibrous/UiO-66 composite for arterial pulse monitoring [348]. The UiO-66- based sensor was obtained by electrospinning PVDF/ UiO-66 suspension onto an aluminum collecting drum, followed by sandwiching the collected composite between two Cu-sputtered aluminum foils. The resulting sensor exhibited a peak-to-peak voltage of $600~\mathrm{{mV}}$ under an applied force of $5\mathrm{~N~}$ . An output voltage of $568~\mathrm{mV}$ was obtained when monitoring wrist artery pulse signal, indicating the promising capability of UiO-66 as a lightweight and flexible material for self-powered wearable electronic devices (Figs. 9(c)–9(e)). Wang et al. presented a highly sensitive, breathable, and flexible pressure sensor by sandwiching nanocomposites of carbonized MOFs and polyaniline nanofiber on a polyurethane sponge (C-MOF/ $\mathrm{PANIF}@\mathrm{PU})$ between two fabric layers [350]. The resulting sensor exhibited a wide sensing range up to $60\ \mathrm{kPa}$ , high sensitivity of $158.26~\mathrm{kPa^{-1}}$ , and fast response/recovery time of $22~\mathrm{ms}/20~\mathrm{ms}$ . The nearly unchanged resistance of the MOFs was observed after bending at $90^{\circ}$ for 1,000 cycles, indicating excellent viability and stability against mechanical bending (Figs. 9(f)–9(i)). The flexible pressure sensors integrated with MOFs have shown their excellent sensitivity and unique mechanics that are compatible with soft, curvilinear tissues. It can be expected that flexible biochemical sensors that use MOFs thin films as functional materials can also be achieved through similar configurations and fabrication approaches, suggesting promising use of such sensors for wearable and implantable biochemical sensing with excellent sensing performance and compatible mechanical properties.
Comparing with lots of demonstrations in physical sensing, works related to the integration of MOFs with flexible electronics for biochemical sensors are still quite limited. Despite several demonstrations of the emerging MOFs-based flexible chemical sensor for gas [345] and pesticide residues [347] sensing, their capability in biochemical sensing for health monitoring is still in an early stage. Liu and coworkers proposed a coffee ringinspired strategy toward oriented self-assembly of a $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{NDC})_{2}$ film on a piece of pencil drawing hydrophobic paper (PDHP) for electrochemical sensing [353]. The sensor has been used to measure lactate and glucose with sensitivities of 0.0359 and
$1.69\mathrm{\mA{\cdot}m^{-2}{\cdot}m M^{-1}}$ , respectively. The detection limits were $25\times$ $10^{-6}\mathrm{M}$ for lactate and $2\times10^{-6}\mathrm{M}$ for glucose. After 200 bending cycles at a bending angle of $180^{\circ}$ , the amperometric responses of the sensor remained $93.1\%$ and $92.5\%$ for lactate and glucose, respectively (Figs. 10(a)–10(d)), indicating that bending-induced mechanical stress did not degrade the sensing performance due to excellent flexibility of PDHP and strong adhesion between PDHP and $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{NDC})_{2}$ . A novel ratiometric sensor that used cotton as a flexible host material, DUT-101 as a reference fluorescence center, and $\mathrm{Ag^{+}/E u^{3+}}@\mathrm{UiO-}67$ as a working fluorescence center has been developed to monitor $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ in sweat [346]. The resulting sensor showed a LOD of $0.1\mathrm{mM}$ and a RSD of $1.4\%$ and $1.2\%$ for 10 repeated tests of 50 and $120\mathrm{mM}$ of $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ , respectively (Figs. $10(\mathrm{e})\substack{-10(\mathrm{g})}$ . Works by our group yielded novel flexible electronic sensors integrated with Cu-MOF or Co-MOF for implantable chemical sensing [7, 319]. The screen-printed flexible sensors modified with MOFs can detect important biomolecules such as ascorbic acid (AA), glycine, l-tryptophan, and glucose with detection limits of 14.97, 0.71, 4.14, and $54.60~\upmu\mathrm{M},$ , respectively, while maintaining stable sensing capability even under extreme deformation and long-term measurement up to 20 days (Figs. 10(h)–10(j)). These results demonstrate promising approaches to integrate MOFs with flexible electronics to realize long-term stable monitoring of chemicals for applications in healthcare, disease diagnosis, and fundamental medical research.
## 4 Discussion and outlook
This review systematically presented the state-of-the-art techniques of MOFs-based biochemical sensors and discussed the feasibility of using flexible electronic devices integrated with MOFs for in-vivo biochemical sensing. The unique characteristics (e.g., large surface areas, tunable pore sizes, and high porosity) and the sensing mechanisms of MOFs indicate that MOFs-based sensors are suitable to conduct long-term, stable biosensing for biomolecules such as $\mathrm{\pH_{\mathrm{:}}}$ , ions, glucose, and cancer cells in biological environment. The enrichment of analytes and the enhancement of selectivity by introducing tunable frameworks are preferable for the complex implantable environment that contains small amounts of analytes and large amounts of interferences. Despite only a few MOFs-based flexible sensors have been demonstrated, the fabrication approaches of these sensors are compatible to both complementary metaloxide semiconductor (CMOS) and printing technologies of flexible electronics, paving the way for integrating MOFs onto flexible substrates for in-vivo biochemical sensing. The downsizing mechanisms allow the achievement of MOFs NPs and thin films that can be readily integrated with other flexible components to yield flexible sensors with excellent mechanics. To further promote the development of MOFs-based flexible sensors, there are still many challenges remained to be solved.
Firstly, the performance of the sensor can be easily influenced by the environment. Moisture and ions may damage metal– ligand bonding, resulting in decay, even collapse of frameworks. Thus, novel compositions and structures of MOFs together with their synthesis approaches should be developed to achieve excellent water stability and the capability to withstand interferences. Some strategies involve introducing high-valence metals, supplying nitrogen-donors, and using hydrophobic ligands. These strategies have led to MOFs such as UiO-66 [417] and MIL-101 [418] that possess excellent water stability. Besides, the selectivity towards analytes can be readily improved by tuning the pore sizes that match the targeting analytes, allowing efficient obstruction of other molecules with larger sizes [419]. Notable examples involve Y-1, 4-NDC-MOF [420] and NJUBai7 [421] that show a significant preference of analytes due to selectivity of pore sizes. Another strategy that can enhance the selectivity is to tune the compositions of MOFs to allow specific affinity to analytes or a high overlap between the emission spectrum of MOFs and the absorption spectrum of analytes [422, 423]. In addition, when comparing with large amounts of chemicals to be detected, only a few MOFs are available to be used. The synergistic selectivity of pore sizes and metal-ligand compositions as well as post-synthesis methods still needs further exploration. Furthermore, although in-vivo toxicity experiments showed that MOFs possessed low toxicity towards cells and tissues for up to 30 days [424, 425], many MOFs contain noble metals (e.g., Au and Pt), rare-earth metals (e.g., Eu, Gd and Tb), and other potentially harmful components (e.g., N,N-dimethylaniline). Thus the biological safety of MOFs for implantable applications needs further evaluation. Approaches for improving the biocompatibility involve tuning the size of MOFs [250–252], introducing nontoxic, green organic ligands (e.g., peptides, amino acids, carbohydrates, and cyclodextrins) [241–243], integrating with other materials (e.g., chitosan, polypyrrole, and polycaprolactone) to form composites [426–428], and developing novel integration approaches with reliable adhesion and capsulation.
Secondly, the integration of MOFs with flexible electronic devices is still at very early stages. Existing MOFs-based flexible chemical sensors were mostly based on electrochemical detection that used simple flexible electrodes modified with MOFs thin films. Since flexible electronics is a very sophisticated technology that involves broad ranges of materials and devices, semiconductor devices such as electro-optical sensors, fieldeffect transistors as well as flexible mechanical devices such as microcantilevers and microbalances have not yet been implemented in the MOFs-based detection. The combination of flexible devices and MOFs may yield new concepts of flexible electronic devices with unique structures and novel mechanisms based on fluorescence quenching, bioaffinity binding, and surface adsorption between MOFs and analytes, leading to diverse flexible sensors based on different sensing principles to meet various demands in healthcare monitoring and disease diagnosis.
With the in-depth cooperation between MOFs and flexible electronics, it is expected to yield long-term stable and ultrasensitive flexible electronics for in-vivo biochemical sensing. These electronic devices may possess highly stable performance even in complex biological environment with only trace of analytes, allowing closed-loop regulation for biological processes and revealing their underlying mechanisms by combining functions such as chemical sensing, drug releasing, filtration, and treatment.
